module

public import Rule30Open.Columns.NaturalTrapsLarge

/-!
# The natural traps of 12 more blocks of periods 13 and 14, of more than 3,000 states, checked in parts

The natural scan of periods 13 and 14 with windows of 16 cells finds, for 8 blocks of period 13 and 4 of period 14,
that every sampled right half driven by the block is inside one trap of 3,295 to 18,132 states at step
12,000, the closure of its window under both values of the cell beyond. [NaturalTrapsLong](NaturalTrapsLong.md) leaves
these out for their size, and the census of locked windows gives each block a smaller trap carried from farther left
([CensusTraps13](CensusTraps13.md), [CensusTraps14](CensusTraps14.md)), which small halves enter less. Here each
natural trap is checked as [NaturalTrapsLarge](NaturalTrapsLarge.md) checks its own: the packed numbers in pieces of
65,536 bits, and the check in parts of 1,024 states, one kernel check each.

* `nat<block>`, `nat<block>_<part>`, `nat<block>_parts`, `nat<block>_check`: the traps, their parts, and the check of
  [WordTraps](WordTraps.md) for each.
* `naturalTrapsLongLarge`, `naturalTrapsLongLarge_check`, `naturalTrapsLongLarge_blocks`: the list, its check and its
  blocks.
* `naturalLongLarge_absorption_excludes`: for each of them, if every finitely supported right half driven by the block
  enters its trap, the center column does not eventually repeat the block.

The traps and their parts come from `python -m computations.lock_chain --lean-long-large`. The natural-language
argument is `NaturalTrapsLongLarge.md`, beside this file.
-/

@[expose] public section

-- Sequential elaboration: the kernel checks would otherwise run in parallel and stack their memory.
set_option Elab.async false

namespace Rule30Open

open Ruliology.CellularAutomata

